The clean checkpoint reopens with all 8,301 original observations, four intended phases, and no frozen variables (Rwp 3.377%). I’m now running the mandated one-site-at-a-time heavy-atom coordinate test; only replay-stable gains with sub-0.15 Å moves and passing contact checks can survive.

The coordinate challenge accepted W1/W2 in WO₃, Pb²⁺ in Pb₃O₄, and Pb in massicot; each gain survived reopening, all moves were under 0.10 Å, and no new short-contact or frozen-variable issue appeared. Rwp fell to 3.192%. The symmetry-fixed Pb/W sites in scheelite and Pb⁴⁺ in minium were correctly rejected as unsupported.

The adopted project has now been replayed twice from fresh reopenings. It is stable at Rwp 3.19224%, has no frozen variables, and its 8,301-angle grid is bit-for-bit identical to the input. Final crystalline-normalized fractions are approximately 56.70% PbWO₄, 21.19% WO₃, 17.06% Pb₃O₄, and 5.04% massicot PbO.

